<strong>Exploring the Limit of Outcome Reward for Learning Mathematical Rea=
soning</strong></em><strong>, https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06781</strong></p>=
<p>This paper explores how far RL with just binary "correct" or "wrong" fee=
dback (like in DeepSeek-R1) can go for solving math problems. To do this, t=
hey start by using Best-of-N sampling to collect positive examples and appl=
y behavior cloning on them, which they show is theoretically enough to opti=
mize the policy.</p><p>To deal with the challenge of sparse rewards (especi=
ally when long chains of thought include partially correct steps) they add =
a token-level reward model that learns to assign importance weights to diff=
erent parts of the reasoning. This helps the model focus on the most critic=
al steps when learning and improves the overall performance.</p><div class=
